{2}(4k-3).                                   \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nUsing (B) together with (2),\n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2k-\\lambda^{2}(4k-3)\\,}\n       =\\sqrt{\\,2k\\bigl(1-2\\lambda^{2}\\bigr)+3\\lambda^{2}\\,}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence the ordinary sum after the even turn \\(2k\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{2k}\n   &= S_{2k-1}+x_{2k}\n    = \\lambda (2k-1)\n      +\\sqrt{\\,2k\\bigl(1-2\\lambda^{2}\\bigr)+3\\lambda^{2}\\,}.   \\tag{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Alice’s winning region  \n\nAlice wins as soon as for some even \\(n=2k\\)\n\n\\[\nQ_{2k}>2k .\n\\]\n\nBecause of (B) we have \\(Q_{2k}=2k\\); therefore it suffices that\n\\(Q_{2k-1}>2k\\).  Using (2),\n\n\\[\n\\lambda^{2}(4k-3)>2k\n\\iff \n\\lambda^{2}>\\frac{2k}{4k-3}.                                   \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe right–hand side decreases with \\(k\\) and tends to \\(\\tfrac12\\).\nConsequently there exists an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5) **iff**\n\\(\\lambda^{2}>\\tfrac12\\), i.e. \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n*If \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)*, Alice follows the maximal strategy (A).\nFor a sufficiently large \\(k\\) inequality (5) holds, so after her\n\\(k\\)-th odd move we have \\(Q_{2k-1}>2k\\); Bazza cannot satisfy the\ncondition \\(Q_{2k}\\le 2k\\) and therefore loses.  Hence Alice has a\nwinning strategy.\n\n*If \\(\\lambda\\le\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)*, the inequality (5) never holds,\nso with the maximal strategy Alice can never force \\(Q_{2k-1}>2k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  Bazza’s winning region  \n\nBazza wins as soon as for some odd \\(n=2k+1\\)\n\n\\[\nS_{2k}> \\lambda(2k+1).                                         \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUsing (4) with \\(k\\) replaced by \\(k\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nS_{2k}= \\lambda (2k-1)\n        +\\sqrt{\\,2k\\bigl(1-2\\lambda^{2}\\bigr)+3\\lambda^{2}\\,}.   \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sqrt{\\,2k\\bigl(1-2\\lambda^{2}\\bigr)+3\\lambda^{2}\\,}\\ge\n\\sqrt{3}\\,\\lambda\\) (the expression under the root is at least\n\\(3\\lambda^{2}\\)), we have the lower bound\n\n\\[\nS_{2k}\\ge \\lambda(2k-1)+\\sqrt3\\,\\lambda\n        =\\lambda\\bigl(2k-1+\\sqrt3\\bigr).                        \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNow compare (8) with the right–hand side of (6):\n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\bigl(2k-1+\\sqrt3\\bigr) > \\lambda(2k+1)\n\\iff \\sqrt3 > 2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is true.  Hence for **every** \\(k\\)\n\n\\[\nS_{2k} > \\lambda(2k+1).\n\\]\n\nConsequently after the even turn \\(2k\\) the ordinary sum already\nexceeds the bound that Alice must respect on the next odd turn; she\ncannot choose any non‑negative \\(x_{2k+1}\\) and loses.\n\nThus, when \\(\\lambda\\le\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) Bazza can adopt the maximal\nstrategy (B).  No matter how Alice plays, after each even turn the sum\nis already larger than \\(\\lambda\\) times the next odd index, so Alice\nfails at her next move.  Hence Bazza has a winning strategy.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\n* If \\(\\displaystyle \\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) Alice can force the\n  square‑sum to exceed the allowed bound and wins.\n\n* If \\(\\displaystyle \\lambda\\le\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) Bazza can keep the\n  square‑sum low while making the ordinary sum exceed Alice’s bound,\n  and he wins.\n\nTherefore the set of \\(\\lambda\\) for which Alice has a winning strategy\nis \\((\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\infty)\\), and the set for which Bazza has a\nwinning strategy is \\([0,\\frac1{\\sqrt2}]\\).  ∎"
