[\n   2\\lambda-\\sqrt{\\,2-y_k^{2}\\,}<0\\iff y_k<\\sqrt{\\,2-4\\lambda^{2}\\,}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Monotonicity of the recurrence.**  \n   Solving \\(y=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-y^{2}}\\) gives the fixed points\n   \\[\n   y=\\lambda\\pm\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}} .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   * If \\(\\lambda>1\\) the quadratic has no real root; then \\(y_{k+1}>y_k\\) for all \\(k\\) (the map is strictly increasing).  \n   * If \\(\\lambda=1\\) the unique fixed point is \\(y=1\\) and the sequence is constant.  \n   * If \\(0<\\lambda<1\\) the map has two real fixed points; the initial value \\(y_1=\\lambda\\) lies strictly between them, whence \\(y_{k+1}<y_k\\) for all \\(k\\) (the sequence is strictly decreasing).\n\n6. **Analysis of the three \u03bb\u2011intervals.**  \n\n   *\\(\\lambda>1\\).*  \n   The sequence \\((y_k)\\) is increasing and bounded above by \\(\\sqrt2\\). Since \\(y_1=\\lambda>1\\), after finitely many steps we obtain \\(y_k>\\sqrt2\\). By (2) Alice\u2019s win condition is met, so Alice has a winning strategy.\n\n   *\\(0\\le\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).*  \n   Here the lower bound in (2) is a positive number:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{\\,2-4\\lambda^{2}\\,}>0 .\n   \\]\n   The sequence \\((y_k)\\) is decreasing and, because the fixed\u2011point equation (3) has no solution in \\([0,\\lambda]\\), it converges to \\(0\\). Consequently there exists a finite index \\(k\\) with\n   \\(y_k<\\sqrt{\\,2-4\\lambda^{2}\\,}\\); at that step the slack for Alice becomes negative, so Bazza wins. Hence Bazza possesses a winning strategy.\n\n   *\\(\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\).*  \n   For \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) the lower bound in (2) equals \\(0\\); for \\(\\lambda> \\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) it is imaginary, so Alice never loses.  \n   The sequence is decreasing (or constant when \\(\\lambda=1\\)) and stays in the interval \\([0,\\sqrt2]\\); it never exceeds \\(\\sqrt2\\), so Alice cannot force a win.  \n   Therefore, with the maximal strategy (A) Alice can always move, and Bazza cannot force a loss. Both players can prevent the other from winning, and the game can be made to continue indefinitely.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The optimal play described above yields the complete classification stated in the answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i ,\\qquad Q_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe rules are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd: } S_n\\le\\lambda n,\\\\[1mm]\n\\text{if }n\\text{ is even: } Q_n\\le n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{0}\n\\]\n\nA player loses as soon as he cannot choose a non\u2011negative \\(x_n\\) satisfying the appropriate inequality.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Maximal admissible moves  \n\n*Alice (odd turn \\(n=2k-1\\)).*  \nShe may increase the sum by at most the remaining amount allowed by her linear bound:\n\\[\nx_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)-S_{2k-2}\\quad(\\ge0).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nWith this choice \\(S_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)\\); any smaller choice would make the sum of squares smaller and can only hinder Alice\u2019s aim of violating Bazza\u2019s bound. Hence (1) is optimal for Alice.\n\n*Bazza (even turn \\(n=2k\\)).*  \nHe may increase the sum of squares by at most the remaining amount allowed by his quadratic bound:\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2k-Q_{2k-1}\\,}\\quad(\\ge0).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nWith this choice \\(Q_{2k}=2k\\); any smaller choice would make the ordinary sum smaller and can only hinder Bazza\u2019s aim of violating Alice\u2019s bound. Hence (2) is optimal for Bazza.\n\nConsequently, if both players follow (1)\u2013(2) the game proceeds as long as the quantities that appear under the square roots stay non\u2011negative. Any other admissible move can only make a player\u2019s own winning condition harder to achieve, so a winning strategy (if it exists) can be realised by the maximal moves.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reduction to a one\u2011dimensional recurrence  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ny_k:=x_{2k-1}\\qquad(k\\ge1),\\qquad\nz_k:=x_{2k}\\qquad(k\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{2k-1}&=\\lambda(2k-1),\\\\\nQ_{2k-1}&=2(k-1)+y_k^{2},\\\\\nz_k&=\\sqrt{\\,2k-Q_{2k-1}\\,}\n      =\\sqrt{\\,2-y_k^{2}\\,},\\\\\nS_{2k}&=S_{2k-1}+z_k\n      =\\lambda(2k-1)+\\sqrt{\\,2-y_k^{2}\\,}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe next odd move is\n\n\\[\ny_{k+1}= \\lambda(2k+1)-S_{2k}\n        =2\\lambda-\\sqrt{\\,2-y_k^{2}\\,}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the whole game is described by the recurrence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,y_{k+1}=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{\\,2-y_k^{2}\\,},\\qquad y_1=\\lambda\\,}.\n\\tag{R}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Winning conditions expressed through \\(y_k\\)\n\n*Alice.*  \nBazza must satisfy \\(Q_{2k}\\le2k\\). Since \\(Q_{2k}=Q_{2k-1}+z_k^{2}=2k\\), the only way he can lose is at the **next** even turn, i.e. when\n\\[\nQ_{2k-1}>2k\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y_k^{2}>2.\n\\]\nHence Alice wins iff for some \\(k\\)\n\\[\n\\boxed{y_k>\\sqrt2.}\n\\tag{A}\n\\]\n\n*Bazza.*  \nAlice must satisfy \\(S_{2k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)\\). Because\n\\[\nS_{2k+1}=S_{2k}+y_{k+1}\n        =\\lambda(2k-1)+\\sqrt{\\,2-y_k^{2}\\,}+y_{k+1},\n\\]\nthe slack for Alice\u2019s next move equals\n\\[\n\\lambda(2k+1)-S_{2k}=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{\\,2-y_k^{2}\\,}=y_{k+1}.\n\\]\nIf this slack is negative, Alice cannot move and loses. Therefore Bazza wins iff for some \\(k\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y_{k+1}<0\\iff 2\\lambda-\\sqrt{\\,2-y_k^{2}\\,}<0\n       \\iff y_k<\\sqrt{\\,2-4\\lambda^{2}\\,}.}\n\\tag{B}\n\\]\n\nThe quantity \\(\\sqrt{\\,2-4\\lambda^{2}\\,}\\) is real exactly when \\(\\lambda\\le\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Behaviour of the recurrence  \n\nSolve the fixed\u2011point equation \\(y=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-y^{2}}\\). Squaring gives\n\n\\[\ny^{2}-2\\lambda y+(2\\lambda^{2}-1)=0,\n\\]\nhence the (real) fixed points are\n\n\\[\ny=\\lambda\\pm\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}}.\n\\tag{F}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(\\lambda>1\\)* the discriminant is negative; (R) has **no** real fixed point.  \nFor any \\(y\\in[0,\\sqrt2]\\),\n\n\\[\ny_{k+1}-y_k=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-y_k^{2}}-y_k>0,\n\\]\nso the sequence \\((y_k)\\) is strictly increasing.\n\n*If \\(\\lambda=1\\)* the quadratic has the double root \\(y=1\\); (R) yields the constant sequence \\(y_k\\equiv1\\).\n\n*If \\(0<\\lambda<1\\)* the quadratic has two distinct real roots\n\\[\ny_{-}=\\lambda-\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}}<0,\\qquad\ny_{+}=\\lambda+\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}}\\in(0,\\sqrt2].\n\\]\nSince \\(y_1=\\lambda\\) satisfies \\(y_{-}<y_1<y_{+}\\) and the map\n\\(f(y)=2\\lambda-\\sqrt{2-y^{2}}\\) is increasing, we have\n\\(f(y)<y\\) for all \\(y\\in(y_{-},y_{+})\\). Consequently\n\\(y_{k+1}<y_k\\) for every \\(k\\); the sequence is strictly decreasing.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Determination of the winning intervals  \n\n**(i)  \\(\\lambda>1\\).**  \nThe sequence \\((y_k)\\) is increasing and bounded above by \\(\\sqrt2\\).  \nBecause \\(y_1=\\lambda>1\\), after finitely many steps we obtain \\(y_k>\\sqrt2\\); by (A) Alice forces Bazza\u2019s failure.  \nHence Alice has a winning strategy for all \\(\\lambda>1\\).\n\n**(ii)  \\(0\\le\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).**  \nHere the lower bound in (B) is a positive number\n\\(\\beta:=\\sqrt{\\,2-4\\lambda^{2}\\,}>0\\).  \nThe sequence \\((y_k)\\) is decreasing and, as shown above, has no fixed point in \\([0,\\lambda]\\); therefore \\(y_k\\to0\\).  \nSince \\(\\beta>0\\), there exists a finite index \\(k\\) with \\(y_k<\\beta\\); then (B) holds and Bazza wins.  \nThus Bazza possesses a winning strategy for every \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n**(iii)  \\(\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\).**  \n\n*For \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)* the bound in (B) equals \\(0\\); the recurrence (R) yields a decreasing sequence converging to \\(0\\), never becoming negative. Hence Alice never loses.  \n*For \\(\\frac1{\\sqrt2}<\\lambda<1\\)* the bound in (B) is imaginary, so Alice\u2019s slack is always non\u2011negative; she can always move.  \nIn"
