mal\u2019\u2019 strategies the sequence \\((d_k)\\) obeys the one\u2011dimensional recurrence (5) as long as \\(d_k\\le2\\lambda\\); if ever \\(d_k>2\\lambda\\) Alice loses by (1).\n\n4. **Fixed points of \\(f\\).**  \n   Solving \\(d=f(d)\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (d-\\lambda)^{2}=1-\\lambda^{2}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   - If \\(\\lambda\\le1\\) there are two real solutions  \n\n     \\[\n     d_{\\pm}= \\lambda\\pm\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}}.\n     \\]\n\n     The larger one \\(d_{+}\\) lies in \\([0,2\\lambda]\\) and is attractive; the smaller one \\(d_{-}\\) is negative (hence irrelevant).  \n   - If \\(\\lambda>1\\) equation (6) has no real solution; consequently \\(f(d)<d\\) for every \\(d\\in(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2,2\\lambda]\\).\n\n5. **Three regimes.**  \n\n   *\\(\\mathbf{\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}}\\).*  \n   For such \\(\\lambda\\) we have \\(d_{+}>2\\lambda\\). Since \\(f(d)>d\\) on the whole interval \\([0,2\\lambda]\\), the sequence \\((d_k)\\) (starting from \\(d_1=\\sqrt{2-\\lambda^{2}}\\)) is strictly increasing and must eventually leave \\([0,2\\lambda]\\). The first index with \\(d_k>2\\lambda\\) makes Alice lose by (1). Hence Bazza\u2019s maximal\u2011square strategy is a winning strategy for every \\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\).\n\n   *\\(\\mathbf{\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le1}\\).*  \n   Here the attractive fixed point \\(d_{+}= \\lambda+\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}}\\) satisfies \\(d_{+}\\le2\\lambda\\). Starting from any admissible \\(d_1\\in[0,\\sqrt2]\\) the iteration (5) converges monotonically to \\(d_{+}\\); consequently \\(d_k\\le2\\lambda\\) for all \\(k\\) and Alice never loses. Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   Q_{2k-1}=2k-d_k^{2}<2k\\qquad(\\forall k),\n   \\]\n\n   so Bazza never loses either. Thus both players can follow their maximal strategies and the game continues forever; no player possesses a winning strategy.\n\n   *\\(\\mathbf{\\lambda>1}\\).*  \n   In this range (6) has no solution, and for every \\(d\\in(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2,2\\lambda]\\) we have \\(f(d)<d\\). Hence the sequence \\((d_k)\\) is strictly decreasing and bounded below by \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2\\); it converges to this lower bound. Consequently there exists a finite \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   d_k\\le 2\\lambda-\\sqrt2,\n   \\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\n   2\\lambda-d_k\\ge\\sqrt2.\n   \\]\n\n   At the following odd turn Alice can choose  \n\n   \\[\n   x_{2k+1}=2\\lambda-d_k\\ge\\sqrt2,\n   \\]\n\n   which yields  \n\n   \\[\n   Q_{2k+1}=Q_{2k}+x_{2k+1}^{2}=2k+(2\\lambda-d_k)^{2}>2(k+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Hence Bazza cannot satisfy his square\u2011sum bound on his next turn and loses.  \n   (If \\(\\lambda>\\sqrt2\\) Alice wins even earlier: \\(x_1=\\lambda\\) gives \\(Q_1=\\lambda^{2}>2\\).)\n\n   Therefore Alice\u2019s maximal\u2011sum strategy guarantees a win for every \\(\\lambda>1\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The three disjoint intervals described above exhaust all positive real \\(\\lambda\\).  \n\n   - \\(0<\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\): Bazza has a winning strategy.  \n   - \\(1/\\sqrt2\\le\\lambda\\le1\\): neither player can force a win; the game can be made infinite.  \n   - \\(\\lambda>1\\): Alice has a winning strategy.\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i,\\qquad Q_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(x_i\\ge0\\) chosen alternately by Alice (odd \\(i\\)) and Bazza (even \\(i\\)).  \nThe rules are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{Alice\u2019s move }(n\\text{ odd}):& S_n\\le\\lambda n,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{Bazza\u2019s move }(n\\text{ even}):& Q_n\\le n.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{A}\n\\]\n\nA player loses on his turn precisely when the opponent\u2019s previous move has already made the relevant inequality impossible, because the current player may only add a non\u2011negative number.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Loss conditions in terms of the \u201cgap\u2019\u2019  \n\nDefine for each integer \\(k\\ge1\\)\n\n\\[\nd_k:=\\sqrt{\\,2k-Q_{2k-1}\\,}\\ge0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n\\(d_k\\) measures how many square\u2011units Bazza may still add on his turn \\(2k\\).  \nIf Bazza uses the maximal admissible choice\n\n\\[\nx_{2k}=d_k,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nthen \\(Q_{2k}=2k\\) and \\(S_{2k}=S_{2k-1}+d_k\\).\n\nAlice\u2019s next move must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k-1}+d_k+x_{2k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1),\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nhence the largest admissible addition is  \n\n\\[\na_k:=x_{2k+1}= \\max\\{0,\\,2\\lambda-d_k\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(d_k>2\\lambda\\) the right\u2013hand side of (3) is negative, so Alice cannot move and loses.  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Alice loses on turn }2k+1\\iff d_k>2\\lambda.}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Recurrence for the gap under maximal play  \n\nWhen Alice uses the maximal addition (4) and Bazza uses (2), after Alice\u2019s move we have  \n\n\\[\nQ_{2k+1}=Q_{2k}+a_k^{2}=2k+(2\\lambda-d_k)^{2},\n\\]\n\nso the new gap is  \n\n\\[\nd_{k+1}= \\sqrt{\\,2(k+1)-Q_{2k+1}\\,}\n       =\\sqrt{\\,2-(2\\lambda-d_k)^{2}\\,}\n       =:f(d_k),\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nf(d)=\\sqrt{\\,2-(d-2\\lambda)^{2}\\,},\\qquad d\\in[0,2\\lambda+\\sqrt2].\n\\]\n\nEquation (6) holds as long as \\(d_k\\le2\\lambda\\); if at some step \\(d_k>2\\lambda\\) Alice has already lost by (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Fixed points of \\(f\\)  \n\nSolving \\(d=f(d)\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n(d-\\lambda)^{2}=1-\\lambda^{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(\\lambda\\le1\\)*, (7) has the two real solutions  \n\n\\[\nd_{\\pm}= \\lambda\\pm\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}}.\n\\]\n\n\\(d_{-}<0\\) and is irrelevant; the attractive fixed point is  \n\n\\[\nd_{+}= \\lambda+\\sqrt{1-\\lambda^{2}}\\in[0,2\\lambda].\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(\\lambda>1\\)*, the right\u2013hand side of (7) is negative, so (7) has no real solution; consequently \\(f(d)\\neq d\\) for every real \\(d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Analysis of the three regimes  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### (i)  \\(\\displaystyle 0<\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nFor such \\(\\lambda\\) we have \\(d_{+}>2\\lambda\\).  \nSince \\(f\\) is increasing on \\([0,2\\lambda]\\) and \\(f(d)>d\\) for every \\(d\\in[0,2\\lambda]\\) (there is no fixed point in this interval), the sequence \\((d_k)\\) generated by (6) is strictly increasing as long as it stays inside \\([0,2\\lambda]\\). Because it cannot converge to a point \\(\\le2\\lambda\\) (no fixed point), it must eventually leave the interval, i.e. some \\(d_k>2\\lambda\\). By (5) Alice then loses.  \n\nHence Bazza\u2019s maximal\u2011square strategy forces a win for every \\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### (ii)  \\(\\displaystyle \\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\)\n\nNow the attractive fixed point \\(d_{+}\\) given by (8) satisfies \\(d_{+}\\le2\\lambda\\).  \nFor any admissible initial value \\(d_1\\in[0,\\sqrt2]\\) the iteration (6) converges monotonically to \\(d_{+}\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nd_k\\le2\\lambda\\qquad(\\forall k),\n\\]\n\nso Alice never loses (by (5)).  \nMoreover  \n\n\\[\nQ_{2k-1}=2k-d_k^{2}<2k\\qquad(\\forall k),\n\\]\n\nso Bazza never loses either.  \n\nThus both players can follow their maximal strategies and the game proceeds forever; no player possesses a winning strategy when \\(\\lambda\\in[1/\\sqrt2,1]\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### (iii)  \\(\\displaystyle \\lambda>1\\)\n\nEquation (7) has no real solution, and for every \\(d\\in(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2,2\\lambda]\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nf(d)<d.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(f(2\\lambda)=\\sqrt2<2\\lambda\\) and \\(f(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2)=0<2\\lambda-\\sqrt2\\); since \\(f\\) is increasing, (9) holds on the whole interval.  \n\nStarting from \\(d_1=\\sqrt{2-\\lambda^{2}}\\) (which lies in \\((2\\lambda-\\sqrt2,2\\lambda]\\) because \\(\\lambda>1\\)), the recurrence (6) produces a strictly decreasing sequence bounded below by \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}d_k=2\\lambda-\\sqrt2.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nConsequently there exists a finite index \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\nd_k\\le2\\lambda-\\sqrt2,\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\n2\\lambda-d_k\\ge\\sqrt2.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAt Alice\u2019s next odd turn she may choose  \n\n\\[\nx_{2k+1}=2\\lambda-d_k\\ge\\sqrt2,\n\\]\n\nwhich gives  \n\n\\[\nQ_{2k+1}=Q_{2k}+x_{2k+1}^{2}=2k+(2\\lambda-d_k)^{2}>2(k+1).\n\\]\n\nThus Bazza cannot satisfy his square\u2011sum bound on his following move and loses.  \n\n(If \\(\\lambda>\\sqrt2\\) the win is even earlier: Alice\u2019s first move \\(x_1=\\lambda\\) already yields \\(Q_1=\\lambda^{2}>2\\).)\n\nHence Alice\u2019s maximal\u2011sum strategy guarantees a win for every \\(\\lambda>1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final classification  \n\nCombining the three cases we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&0<\\lambda<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy};\\\\[4pt]\n&\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\le\\lambda\\le1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\text{neither player can force a win (the game can last forever)};\\\\[4pt]\n&\\lambda>1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\text{Alice has a winning strategy.}\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
