ative \\(x_{2k+1}\\) with  \n     \\(S_{2k}+x_{2k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)\\), which is impossible; therefore Alice loses and Bazza wins.\n\n4. **Critical case \\(\\lambda=1/\\sqrt2\\).**  \n   * With Alice’s maximal‑sum strategy and Bazza’s zero‑move strategy we obtain  \n     \\[\n     S_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1),\\qquad Q_{2k-1}=2k .\n     \\]  \n   * Both constraints are met with equality at every turn, so the game can continue indefinitely.  \n   * Neither player can force a win, establishing the draw case.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i ,\\qquad   \nQ_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}\\qquad (n\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nThe rules are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{odd }n:\\; S_n\\le\\lambda n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{even }n:\\; Q_n\\le n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Preliminary inequalities  \n\nFor any non‑negative numbers \\(x_1,\\dots ,x_n\\),\n\n\\[\nQ_n\\ge\\frac{S_n^{2}}{n}\\qquad\\text{(Cauchy–Schwarz)},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nand equivalently  \n\n\\[\nS_n\\le\\sqrt{n\\,Q_n}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Alice’s winning strategy for \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nAssume \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).  \n\n**Alice’s move.**  \nOn an odd turn \\(n=2k-1\\) she chooses  \n\n\\[\nx_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)-S_{2k-2}\\ (\\ge0),\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ni.e. she saturates the sum bound (possible because \\(S_{2k-2}\\le\\lambda(2k-1)\\) will be shown).\n\n**Bazza’s response.**  \nBazza may always answer with \\(x_{2k}=0\\).  \nThen \\(Q_{2k}=Q_{2k-1}\\) and the even‑turn condition \\(Q_{2k}\\le2k\\) is preserved as long as it held after the previous odd turn.\n\n**Induction that Alice’s move is always feasible.**  \nBase: before the first turn \\(S_0=0\\le\\lambda\\).  \nInductive step: suppose after turn \\(2k-2\\) we have \\(S_{2k-2}\\le\\lambda(2k-1)\\).  \nBecause Bazza chose \\(x_{2k}=0\\), we also have \\(S_{2k-1}=S_{2k-2}+x_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)\\).  \nThus \\(S_{2k-1}\\) satisfies the odd‑turn bound with equality, and the next even turn starts with \\(S_{2k-1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)\\) (since \\(\\lambda(2k-1)<\\lambda(2k+1)\\)). Hence (4) is well‑defined for all \\(k\\).\n\n**Growth of the square sum.**  \nFrom (4) and \\(x_{2k}=0\\),\n\n\\[\nx_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)-S_{2k-2}\n          =\\lambda(2k-1)-\\lambda(2k-3)=2\\lambda .\n\\]\n\nConsequently each odd turn adds \\((2\\lambda)^2=4\\lambda^{2}\\) to the square sum, while even turns add nothing. After the \\((2k-1)\\)-st turn,\n\n\\[\nQ_{2k-1}=k\\cdot4\\lambda^{2}=4\\lambda^{2}k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n**Winning condition.**  \nIf \\(Q_{2k-1}>2k\\) then Bazza cannot satisfy his even‑turn requirement \\(Q_{2k}\\le2k\\) (any non‑negative \\(x_{2k}\\) would keep \\(Q_{2k}\\ge Q_{2k-1}>2k\\)).  \nFrom (5) the inequality \\(4\\lambda^{2}k>2k\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n4\\lambda^{2}>2\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nHence for \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) there exists a finite \\(k\\) for which \\(Q_{2k-1}>2k\\); Bazza then has no legal move and loses.  \nThus **Alice has a winning strategy** when \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Bazza’s winning strategy for \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nAssume \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).  \n\n**Alice’s optimal (for herself) move.**  \nTo keep the sum as small as possible she may always choose \\(x_{2k-1}=0\\).  \n(This cannot hurt Bazza’s aim of making Alice fail.)\n\n**Bazza’s optimal move.**  \nOn his even turn \\(n=2k\\) he uses the entire remaining square budget in a single number:\n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2k-Q_{2k-1}\\,}\\ (\\ge0).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause Alice has contributed no squares, \\(Q_{2k-1}=0\\) and (6) gives \\(x_{2k}= \\sqrt{2k}\\).  \nThus after the \\(2k\\)-th turn\n\n\\[\nQ_{2k}=2k,\\qquad \nS_{2k}= \\sum_{i=1}^{k}x_{2i}=k\\sqrt{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe choice (6) is admissible: it makes \\(Q_{2k}=2k\\), exactly meeting the even‑turn bound.\n\n**Comparison with Alice’s sum bound.**  \nAt the following odd turn \\(2k+1\\) Alice must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k}+x_{2k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_{2k+1}\\ge0\\), (8) can hold only if  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k}\\le\\lambda(2k+1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we have \\(S_{2k}=k\\sqrt{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\),\n\n\\[\nk\\sqrt{2}> \\lambda(2k+1)\\qquad\\text{for all sufficiently large }k .\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\frac{k\\sqrt{2}}{2k+1}= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot\\frac{k}{k+\\tfrac12}\n\\longrightarrow\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}> \\lambda .\n\\]\n\nHence for some large \\(k\\) inequality (9) fails, making (8) impossible.  \nConsequently Alice has no legal move on turn \\(2k+1\\) and loses.  \n\nThus **Bazza has a winning strategy** when \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The critical case \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nLet \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).  \n\n* Alice follows the maximal‑sum strategy (4).  \n* Bazza answers with \\(x_{2k}=0\\).\n\nThen for every \\(k\\),\n\n\\[\nS_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1),\\qquad \nx_{2k-1}=2\\lambda,\\qquad \nQ_{2k-1}=k\\cdot4\\lambda^{2}=2k .\n\\]\n\nBoth constraints (1) are satisfied with equality at each turn, so the game can continue indefinitely.  \n\nConversely, any deviation from these optimal choices would make one of the two quantities exceed its bound, giving the opponent a win. Hence **neither player possesses a forced win** when \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion  \n\nThe analysis yields a complete classification:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\; \\Longrightarrow\\; \\text{Alice has a winning strategy},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\; \\Longr