ld need  \n     \\[\n     S_{2k}+x_{2k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1).\n     \\]  \n     Because \\(x_{2k+1}\\ge0\\), this requires \\(S_{2k}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)\\), which contradicts (E) for all sufficiently large \\(k\\) when \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) (since \\(\\sqrt{2}>2\\lambda\\)). Hence Alice eventually has no legal move and Bazza wins.\n\n4. **Critical case \\(\\lambda=1/\\sqrt2\\).**  \n   * Alice follows (B) and Bazza answers with \\(x_{2k}=0\\).  \n   * Then for every \\(k\\) we have  \n     \\[\n     S_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1),\\qquad \n     Q_{2k-1}= \\lambda^{2}(4k-3)=2k-\\tfrac32 .\n     \\]  \n   * Both constraints are satisfied (the odd‑turn bound with equality, the even‑turn bound strictly). Hence the game can continue indefinitely; neither player can force a win.\n\nThus the classification above is complete.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i ,\\qquad   \nQ_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}\\qquad (n\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nThe rules are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{odd }n:\\; S_n\\le\\lambda n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{even }n:\\; Q_n\\le n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Preliminary inequalities  \n\nFor any non‑negative numbers \\(x_1,\\dots ,x_n\\),\n\n\\[\nQ_n\\ge\\frac{S_n^{2}}{n}\\qquad\\text{(Cauchy–Schwarz)},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nand equivalently  \n\n\\[\nS_n\\le\\sqrt{n\\,Q_n}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Alice’s winning strategy for \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nAssume \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).  \n\n**Alice’s move.**  \nOn an odd turn \\(n=2k-1\\) she chooses  \n\n\\[\nx_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)-S_{2k-2}\\ (\\ge0).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the odd‑turn bound is met with equality:\n\\(S_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)\\).\n\n**Bazza’s response.**  \nBazza may always answer with \\(x_{2k}=0\\).  \nThen \\(Q_{2k}=Q_{2k-1}\\) and the even‑turn condition \\(Q_{2k}\\le2k\\) is preserved as long as it held after the previous odd turn.\n\n**Feasibility of Alice’s move.**  \nWe prove by induction that (4) is always admissible.\n\n*Base.* \\(S_0=0\\le\\lambda\\), so \\(x_1=\\lambda\\ge0\\).\n\n*Inductive step.* Suppose after turn \\(2k-2\\) we have \\(S_{2k-2}= \\lambda(2k-2)\\) (true for \\(k=1\\)).  \nBecause Bazza chose \\(x_{2k}=0\\), we also have \\(S_{2k-1}=S_{2k-2}+x_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)\\).  \nHence \\(S_{2k-1}\\le\\lambda(2k-1)\\) and the next even turn starts with a sum not exceeding the bound for \\(n=2k\\). Consequently (4) is well‑defined for all \\(k\\).\n\n**Growth of the square sum.**  \nFrom (4) we obtain\n\\[\nx_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)-S_{2k-2}\n        = \\lambda(2k-1)-\\lambda(2k-2)=\\lambda\n\\]\nfor \\(k=1\\), and for \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\\[\nx_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1)-\\lambda(2k-2)=2\\lambda .\n\\]\n\nThus the contribution to \\(Q_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\nx_1^{2}= \\lambda^{2},\\qquad\nx_{2k-1}^{2}= (2\\lambda)^{2}=4\\lambda^{2}\\;(k\\ge2).\n\\]\n\nConsequently after the \\((2k-1)\\)-st turn  \n\n\\[\nQ_{2k-1}= \\lambda^{2}+ (k-1)4\\lambda^{2}\n        = \\lambda^{2}(4k-3).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n**Winning condition.**  \nIf \\(Q_{2k-1}>2k\\) then on the following even turn Bazza cannot satisfy \\(Q_{2k}\\le2k\\) (any non‑negative \\(x_{2k}\\) would keep \\(Q_{2k}\\ge Q_{2k-1}>2k\\)).  \nFrom (5) the inequality \\(Q_{2k-1}>2k\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda^{2}(4k-3)>2k\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n(4\\lambda^{2}-2)k>3\\lambda^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) we have \\(4\\lambda^{2}-2>0\\); therefore the right‑hand side is a fixed positive number and the left‑hand side grows linearly with \\(k\\). Hence there exists an integer \\(k\\) satisfying the inequality, and Bazza eventually has no legal move.  \n\nThus **Alice has a winning strategy** when \\(\\lambda>\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Bazza’s winning strategy for \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nAssume \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).  \n\n**Alice’s optimal (for herself) move.**  \nTo keep the sum as small as possible she may always choose  \n\n\\[\nx_{2k-1}=0\\qquad(k\\ge1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThis never harms Bazza’s aim of making Alice fail.\n\n**Bazza’s optimal move.**  \nOn his even turn \\(n=2k\\) he uses the whole remaining square budget in a single number:\n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2k-Q_{2k-1}\\,}\\ (\\ge0).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince Alice has contributed no squares, \\(Q_{2k-1}=0\\) and (7) gives  \n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{2k}.\n\\]\n\nHence after \\(2k\\) turns  \n\n\\[\nQ_{2k}=2k,\\qquad \nS_{2k}= \\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\sqrt{2i}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n**A lower bound for the sum.**  \nFor every \\(i\\ge1\\), \\(\\sqrt{2i}\\ge\\sqrt{2}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k}= \\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\sqrt{2i}\\ \\ge\\ k\\sqrt{2}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n**Failure of Alice’s next move.**  \nAt the next odd turn \\(2k+1\\) Alice must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k}+x_{2k+1}\\le\\lambda(2k+1).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x_{2k+1}\\ge0\\), (10) can hold only if  \n\n\\[\nS_{2k}\\le\\lambda(2k+1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nUsing (9),\n\n\\[\nk\\sqrt{2}\\le\\lambda(2k+1)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\sqrt{2}\\le2\\lambda+\\frac{\\lambda}{k}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\) we have \\(2\\lambda<\\sqrt{2}\\).  \nThe term \\(\\frac{\\lambda}{k}\\) tends to \\(0\\) as \\(k\\to\\infty\\); consequently (11) is violated for all sufficiently large \\(k\\). At that odd turn Alice has no admissible \\(x_{2k+1}\\) and loses.\n\nThus **Bazza has a winning strategy** when \\(\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The critical case \\(\\displaystyle\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\)\n\nLet \\(\\lambda=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\).  \n\n* Alice follows the maximal‑sum rule (4).  \n* Bazza answers with \\(x_{2k}=0\\).\n\nFrom the computation in Section 2 we obtain for every \\(k\\ge1\\)\n\n\\[\nS_{2k-1}= \\lambda(2k-1),\\q