es \\(x_{2k-1}=0\\) (this is her optimal defence,\nbecause any positive odd move only helps Bazza later).  \nAt an even turn \\(2k\\) Bazza may pick the largest admissible number,\n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{\\,2k-Q_{2k-2}\\,}.\n\\]\n\nIf after turn \\(2k-2\\) we have \\(Q_{2k-2}=2k-2\\) (true for \\(k=1\\) and\npreserved inductively), then\n\n\\[\nx_{2k}= \\sqrt{2k-(2k-2)}=\\sqrt2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently for every \\(k\\ge1\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_{2k}= \\sqrt2 ,\\qquad Q_{2k}=2k ,\\qquad S_{2k}=k\\sqrt2 .}\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. When does Bazza win?\n\nWith Alice\u2019s choice \\(x_{2k+1}=0\\) she survives the next odd turn iff\n\n\\[\nS_{2k}=k\\sqrt2\\le\\lambda(2k+1).\n\\]\n\nIf for some \\(k\\),\n\n\\[\nk\\sqrt2>\\lambda(2k+1), \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAlice cannot select a non\u2011negative \\(x_{2k+1}\\) satisfying the odd\u2011turn\ncondition, so she loses and Bazza wins.\n\nDividing (3) by \\(2k\\) and letting \\(k\\to\\infty\\) gives the necessary\ncondition \\(\\sqrt2>2\\lambda\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\lambda<\\frac1{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nConversely, if \\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\) then for sufficiently large \\(k\\) inequality\n(3) holds (because the left\u2011hand side grows like \\(k\\) while the right\u2011hand side\ngrows like \\(2\\lambda k\\) with a smaller coefficient).  \nThus **Bazza has a winning strategy for every \\(\\lambda<1/\\sqrt2\\)**,\nnamely the strategy described in (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Alice\u2019s winning strategy for \\(\\lambda>1/\\sqrt2\\)\n\nAssume again that Bazza follows the maximal\u2011growth strategy (2); this gives\nAlice the largest possible \u201cslack\u2019\u2019 in her sum constraint.\n\nAfter the even turn \\(2k\\) we have, by (2),\n\n\\[\nS_{2k}=k\\sqrt2,\\qquad Q_{2k}=2k .\n\\]\n\nThe difference between Alice\u2019s allowed sum \\(\\lambda(2k)\\) and the actual sum is\n\n\\[\nL_k:=\\lambda(2k)-S_{2k}=k\\bigl(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2\\bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\lambda>1/\\sqrt2\\), the factor \\(2\\lambda-\\sqrt2\\) is positive, so\n\\(L_k\\) grows linearly with \\(k\\).\n\nOn the following odd turn \\(2k+1\\) Alice chooses\n\n\\[\nx_{2k+1}=L_k .\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n\\[\nS_{2k+1}=S_{2k}+L_k=\\lambda(2k+1),\\qquad\nQ_{2k+1}=Q_{2k}+L_k^{2}=2k+L_k^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor sufficiently large \\(k\\) we have \\(L_k>\\sqrt2\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nQ_{2k+1}=2k+L_k^{2}>2k+2 .\n\\]\n\nThe even\u2011turn condition for the next move (\\(n=2k+2\\)) requires\n\\(Q_{2k+2}\\le 2k+2\\), but \\(Q_{2k+1}\\) already exceeds this bound, and any\nnon\u2011negative addition can only increase it. Hence Bazza has no legal move and\nloses.  \n\nThus **Alice wins for every \\(\\lambda>1/\\sqrt2\\)** by the strategy\n\u201cuse all the slack on the first odd turn after the slack becomes larger than\n\\(\\sqrt2\\)\u201d.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. The critical value \\(\\lambda=1/\\sqrt2\\)\n\nIf \\(\\lambda=1/\\sqrt2\\), then from (4) we obtain \\(L_k\\equiv0\\); the play\ndescribed in Sections\u202f2\u20133 proceeds forever with\n\n\\[\nS_{2k}=k\\sqrt2,\\qquad Q_{2k}=2k,\n\\]\n\nand each player can always choose a legal number (Alice chooses \\(0\\) on odd\nturns, Bazza chooses \\(\\sqrt2\\) on even turns).  \nNo player can force a violation, so **neither player has a winning strategy**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nCollecting the three cases:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Bazza has a winning strategy } &&\\iff\\; \\lambda<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{Neither player can force a win } &&\\iff\\; \\lambda=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{Alice has a winning strategy } &&\\iff\\; \\lambda>\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
